    "contentPolicy": { "class": "dual-use" }

Purpose of this file is to describe, accurately and in full, the
security-relevant capabilities the package ships and the legitimate use
they are intended for.


1. WHAT THIS PACKAGE IS
-----------------------

A plugin for DeepSeek Harness (DSH), a locally-run AI coding assistant
for Windows. It lets the owner of a PC reach their own DSH instance, and
view/control their own desktop, from a phone browser while physically
away from the machine.

This package is a fork of the MIT-licensed package `dsh-remote-desktop`
by xiongjie771017 (https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-remote-desktop).
The dual-use capabilities described in section 2 are inherited from that
upstream package. This fork adds no new capability; see section 6 for the
one behavioural fix it contains.


2. DUAL-USE CAPABILITIES
------------------------

The package contains the following capabilities, which are the reason it
is declared dual-use. All of them act on the end user's own machine, at
the end user's own request, from the end user's own phone.

2.1 Local HTTP reverse proxy  (lib/index.js)
    Binds 127.0.0.1:8090 and forwards requests to the local DSH web
    server. It also routes /remote-* paths to the screen service on
    127.0.0.1:8092. It binds the loopback interface only; it does not
    open a port on any other interface. It is the operator who
    separately configures a tunneling tool (cpolar / frp / ngrok /
    similar) to expose it, if they choose to.

2.2 Remote screen capture  (lib/screen.ps1)
    A PowerShell worker that captures the Windows desktop and encodes
    frames as JPEG, streamed to the connected phone. Capture is
    demand-driven and stops ~3 seconds after the last viewer request.

2.3 Input injection  (lib/index.js, lib/screen.ps1)
    Moves the local mouse cursor, performs clicks, sends keystrokes and
    text, and scrolls, on the local machine. Commands originate from the
    authenticated phone session.

2.4 Local file transfer  (lib/index.js)
    Lists, downloads, uploads and zips files on the local machine.
    By default the browsable root is the user's home directory.

2.5 Optional Windows unlock service  (lib/dsh-unlock-svc.exe,
    lib/dsh-unlock-helper.exe, lib/install-unlock-service.bat,
    lib/task.xml)
    Windows deliberately ignores synthetic input on the lock screen, so
    remote control cannot dismiss it. To work around that, upstream
    ships a small prebuilt Windows service, `DSHRemoteUnlock`, which can
    inject the user's own PIN into the locked session.

    Installing it is MANUAL and requires the operator to double-click
    lib/install-unlock-service.bat and accept a UAC elevation prompt.
    The script then:
      - copies both .exe files into C:\Windows\System32\
      - creates the service with `sc create ... start= auto`
        (LocalSystem account, starts at boot)
      - starts it and verifies it over the named pipe
        `\\.\pipe\dsh-remote-unlock`
    task.xml is an equivalent Task Scheduler definition (runs as
    S-1-5-18, i.e. SYSTEM, at boot) for operators who install it that
    way instead.

    The package never installs this service by itself. Remote screen,
    input and file transfer all work without it; only lock-screen
    unlock and lock-state display require it.

2.6 Bundled Android app  (lib/dsh-remote.apk)
    A prebuilt WebView wrapper, shipped so the operator can install a
    convenience client on their own phone. It points at a URL the
    operator enters themselves. No third-party service is contacted for
    it.

2.7 QR code rendering
    The configuration panel displays QR codes so the operator can open the
    access URL, or download the bundled app, with a phone camera. These
    are generated **locally**: lib/qr.js encodes them with the MIT
    `qrcode` library and the bundled screen server returns the PNG from
    http://127.0.0.1:8092/remote-qr . Nothing is sent to any external
    service.

    Upstream used to load these images from a public QR web service
    (api.qrserver.com), which both depended on reachability of a foreign
    host and disclosed the operator's access URL to it. This fork removed
    that dependency.

    The package therefore makes NO outbound request to any third party.
    The screen, input, file, proxy and QR paths are all loopback-only.


3. INTENDED LEGITIMATE USE
--------------------------

Use by a machine's own owner, on their own machine, to reach it from
their own phone: checking on a long-running build or agent session,
reading the screen, transferring a file, or using their own DSH instance
while away from the desk. It is functionally in the same category as
TeamViewer, AnyDesk, Chrome Remote Desktop, RustDesk and VNC servers,
which are similarly dual-use and similarly shipped as installable
remote-control software.

The package is not intended for, and must not be used for, accessing any
machine the operator does not own or is not authorised to control.


4. WHAT THIS PACKAGE DOES NOT DO
--------------------------------

- It does not bind to any non-loopback interface. Both listeners
  (lib/index.js:1101 and lib/index.js:1105) are explicitly bound to
  "127.0.0.1". Exposure to a network happens only if the operator
  configures a separate tunneling tool to forward to 127.0.0.1:8090.
- It does not install, register or start anything by itself. The
  LocalSystem service in 2.5 exists only if the operator manually runs
  install-unlock-service.bat and approves UAC. Nothing runs as SYSTEM
  from a plain `npm install`.
- It does not run any install lifecycle script. The package declares no
  preinstall / install / postinstall / prepare script.
- It does not collect, harvest or exfiltrate credentials, browser data,
  cookies, tokens or keystrokes. The input-injection path only *sends*
  synthetic keystrokes to the local desktop at the operator's request;
  there is no keylogging or capture path in the codebase.
- It does not contact any command-and-control or analytics endpoint, and
  makes no outbound request to any third party at all. QR codes are
  generated locally (see 2.7).
- It does not obfuscate or encrypt its own code. lib/index.js,
  lib/client.js, lib/qr.js, lib/screen.ps1 and lib/helper.ps1 are plain
  readable JS/PowerShell shipped as-is.
- It does not contain a cryptocurrency miner, ransomware, a
  self-propagation mechanism, or code that acts without operator intent.


5. SECURITY POSTURE (disclosed for completeness)
------------------------------------------------

Reviewed honestly, this package is not hardened:

- The 8090 proxy performs NO authentication of its own. It rewrites
  Host/Origin/Referer and forwards. Anyone who can reach it, can drive
  the machine, including the file-transfer endpoints, which resolve
  arbitrary absolute paths on the local filesystem. The package's README
  therefore instructs the operator to enable authentication on the
  tunneling tool itself (cpolar "Http Auth", frp http_user/http_pwd,
  ngrok --basic-auth) and warns that omitting it exposes the machine's
  screen, input and entire filesystem to the internet.
- The bundled .exe files are prebuilt binaries from upstream. They are
  shipped as-is and cannot be audited from this repository; only the
  .bat installer and task.xml that register them are readable here.
- Screen frames and input commands travel over plain HTTP on loopback
  and are not additionally encrypted by the plugin.


6. FORK CHANGES RELATIVE TO UPSTREAM
------------------------------------

Four changes: one UI rework, one dependency removal, and two bug fixes.
No capability was added or removed.

a) DSH 0.2.x authentication (lib/index.js, marked `[PATCH harness-auth]`)
   Upstream rewrote Host/Origin/Referer and forwarded, with no cookie or
   token handling at all, so DSH 0.2.x's authority-bound signed-cookie
   gate rejected every request arriving via a tunnel with "dsh web
   authentication required". The proxy now redeems a `dsh-auth-*` cookie
   through ctx.connection.authenticatedUrl() and attaches it to
   forwarded requests (HTTP and WebSocket upgrade).

b) Client module identifier (lib/client.js)
   The upstream client bundle registers itself as "dsh-remote-desktop",
   its old package name. DSH requires that identifier to equal the npm
   package name, so with the package renamed the plugin failed to load
   and, on DSH 0.2.0-rc.2, took the host application's startup down with
   it. It is now "dsh-remote-control-desktop".

c) Local QR generation (new lib/qr.js, lib/index.js route /remote-qr)
   Replaces the upstream dependency on a public QR image service, as
   described in 2.7. Adds one dependency, `qrcode` (MIT), used
   host-side only. No new capability: upstream already displayed these
   QR codes, just via a third party.

d) Configuration panel reworked (lib/client.js)
   Upstream showed an always-visible draggable box in the bottom-left
   corner of the page. It is now a "远程访问" entry in the sidebar footer
   that opens a modal panel on click, registered through the host's own
   public slot API (sidebar.footer.action) - the same mechanism the
   session-manager entry uses. If that slot is unavailable the code falls
   back to a DOM button, and finally to a fixed floating button, so the
   entry cannot silently disappear. No capability changed; the same
   controls are present.


